;; C++'s dg check's divergences (doc/cpp/CPP-SURVEY.md, "As built: CX9 (write-ahead)"; bin/repr-cpp dg): the classes every
;; file of g++.dg's dg-error and preprocess parts that does not pass falls into, in test/goi/divergences.edn's format. A
;; file's class is the first whose :match finds "RESULT REASON" as test/cpp/check-dg-linux-amd64.edn (and
;; test/repr/cpp/dg-preprocess-results.edn) write them: "fail KIND: DETAIL", KIND one of missed (an expected error line
;; without an error; "missed: accepted; ..." when the oracle accepted the unit), extra (an error on a line that expects
;; none), both, stopped (errors outside the main file, or the expected lines after a fatal error), refused (repr/cpp's
;; converter refused a unit the oracle accepted), crash and timeout (no class: they fail, but the oracle's own crash, oracle-crash); the preprocess part's kinds are
;; pp-missed, pp-extra, pp-both, pp-stopped (the shared preprocessor's errors). A failure whose directives g++ 15.2 does not
;; give either carries " [g++: [MISSING EXTRA ...]]" (the recorded reference, test/cpp/ref-dg-gxx15.edn).
;; The dg-error part is the oracle's (CP2 (a), CP3 (a)): repr/cpp has no Sema of its own, the errors are clang 23.1.4's
;; and the expectations g++'s, so the clang-* classes are of the design; refused is the converter's gap, gxx-disagrees and
;; prune-output the harness's (deferred: review 196, the user's decision). Read with the Clojure reader.
[
 {:id :prune-output
  :title "a dg-prune-output file with errors the directives do not place"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "DejaGnu removes the diagnostics a dg-prune-output regex matches before it counts excess errors (review 196, finding 5). Since cpp-compl (2026-11-08) the check applies the patterns as gcc-dg.exp's gcc-dg-prune does, to clang's and to g++'s output, after the directives' lines took theirs (arbace.repr.cpp.dg/prune-text, prune-errors): the row says [dg-prune-output N] only for N patterns Java's regexps refuse (none in g++.dg's dg-error files)"
  :decided "CX9 (review 196); applied by cpp-compl"
  :match [#"^fail (pp-)?(missed|extra|both|stopped): .*\[dg-prune-output \d+\]$"]}
 ;; cpp-compl: the target's C library, named by file (bin/reap's :tests)
 {:id :musl-libc
  :title "the target's C library is musl: g++.dg's expectation is glibc's (NULL as __null, iconv failing on an unencodable character)"
  :kind :design
  :why "on x86_64-linux-musl (C's own target, c/README.md) g++'s #include <stddef.h> and <cstddef> find musl's header (/usr/include/stddef.h: `#define NULL nullptr` from C++11), not gcc's ginclude/stddef.h (`#define NULL __null`), so the -Wconversion-null tests' warnings on an integer from NULL are errors, g++ 15.2's own here (\"cannot convert 'std::nullptr_t' to 'int'\") as clang's (warn/Wnull-conversion-1.C, warn/Wconversion-null-4.C, template/nontype10.C); and musl's iconv converts a character the charset cannot encode to '*' instead of failing (EILSEQ), so -fexec-charset=ISO-8859-1's \"converting to execution character set\" errors are not given by g++ here either (cpp26/literals2.C; dg-require-iconv only asks that iconv_open succeeds). The reference errs as the target does, not as the directives say"
  :decided "cpp-compl (2026-11-08): found among gxx-disagrees' files by g++'s reference"
  :tests ["gxx-dg/warn/Wnull-conversion-1.C" "gxx-dg/warn/Wconversion-null-4.C" "gxx-dg/template/nontype10.C" "gxx-dg/cpp26/literals2.C"]
  :match []}
 {:id :gxx-disagrees
  :title "g++ 15.2 does not give the directives' errors either"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the reference, run with the file's options at the file's standard (test/cpp/ref-dg-gxx15.edn), misses an expected line or errs on another: a directive whose selector the targets' tables decide otherwise than DejaGnu on gcc's own build (an effective target taken to hold, test/c/dg-targets.edn), an option the check drops, an xfail DejaGnu counts, a test of another standard than the one the check picks (one of DejaGnu's list, CP210's question): the expectation is not this host's, so the oracle is not judged against it; almost always the harness's model of DejaGnu is wrong for the file (gcc passes its own g++.dg), so the class is a gap to shrink (review 196)"
  :decided "CX9 (CP11 (a): g++ the reference); review 196's decision (the user): the harness's gap, deferred, each cause found fixed or named"
  :match [#"^fail (pp-)?(missed|extra|both|stopped): .*\[g\+\+: \["]}
 {:id :clang-pedantic
  :title "clang's -pedantic-errors rejects an extension of a later standard that g++ only warns about"
  :kind :design
  :why "g++.dg's default flags are -pedantic-errors -Wno-long-long (dg.exp); clang turns its own extension warnings (C++17 attributes in C++11, a statement in a constexpr function before C++23, invalid UTF-8 in a comment ...) into errors, which g++ gives as pedwarns it does not count or does not give"
  :decided "CX9 (CP2 (a): the oracle's diagnostics)"
  :match [#"^fail (extra|both): .*\[-Werror,-W"]}
 {:id :clang-accepts
  :title "the oracle accepts a unit g++ rejects"
  :kind :design
  :why "a check g++ makes and clang does not (gcc's own pedantic and -fpermissive rules, redeclarations of builtins, gcc's extensions' errors, attributes clang ignores), or one an option enables that the check gives g++ alone (-fconcepts, -fcontracts, -fgnu-tm, -fpermissive, -fmodules-ts ...: clang refuses options it does not know, so they are dropped from its arguments); repr/cpp reads the unit (the converter ran), and the read does not reject what Sema accepted"
  :decided "CX9 (CP2 (a), CP3 (a): the oracle decides acceptance)"
  :match [#"^fail missed: accepted; "]}
 {:id :clang-other-lines
  :title "both reject, the errors on other lines"
  :kind :design
  :why "clang reports the error at another line than g++ (the declaration rather than the use, the template definition rather than its instantiation, the token after a missing one), or recovers otherwise and reports a second error g++ does not, missing one g++ gives"
  :decided "CX9 (CP2 (a): positions are the oracle's)"
  :match [#"^fail both: "]}
 {:id :clang-fewer-errors
  :title "both reject, clang gives errors on fewer of the expected lines"
  :kind :design
  :why "clang stops diagnosing a declaration after its first error, folds several of g++'s errors into one, or gives the rest as notes or warnings; the lines it does give are all expected"
  :decided "CX9 (CP2 (a))"
  :match [#"^fail missed: missed "]}
 {:id :clang-extra
  :title "clang errs on a line that expects no error"
  :kind :design
  :why "clang's stricter checks (a narrowing, a missing typename, an incomplete type, a character too large), an error g++ gives as a warning, or a second error at a line after its recovery; every expected line has an error"
  :decided "CX9 (CP2 (a))"
  :match [#"^fail extra: " #"^fail missed: line 0 expects"]}
 {:id :clang-elsewhere
  :title "clang's errors are located outside the main file"
  :kind :design
  :why "an error in an included header (an instantiation inside libstdc++, a test's own .h) that the file's directives do not place: g++ reports the instantiation's error at the main file's line or under a line-0 directive"
  :decided "CX9 (C's stopped verdict)"
  :match [#"^fail stopped: "]}
 {:id :dg-unsupported
  :title "DejaGnu's UNSUPPORTED: a directive's selector is false on this target, or the directory's driver is not modelled"
  :kind :design
  :why "a dg-do, dg-skip-if or dg-require selector false on x86-64 Linux with g++ 15.2 at the standards the run takes (ia32, mingw, darwin, aarch64, ! c++11 ...), or a directory whose .exp driver builds something first (pch, plugin, debug's -g levels, ecos): not a test here, as C's class of the same name (CS195 (a)); counted apart from the passes"
  :decided "CS195 (a) for C; cppi-check (2026-11-05) for C++, CX9's verdict unsupported"
  :match [#"^unsupported"]}
 {:id :read-refused
  :title "repr/cpp's converter refuses a unit the oracle accepts"
  :kind :gap
  :why "a construct the converter has no form for in a macro (CP79 (b)'s refusals) or a gap it reports (:cpp/gap): the read level's open cases (CX4-CX5's classes, test/repr/cpp/roundtrip-known.edn)"
  :decided "CX9; CX5"
  :match [#"^fail refused: "]}
 {:id :oracle-crash
  :title "the oracle crashes on the unit"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "clang 23.1.4 dies by a signal on the unit (cxxdump: \"clang failed (not a rejection)\"): an oracle bug, no judgment; the day's runs: gomp/linear-1.C and gomp/depobj-1.C under -fopenmp, a segmentation fault (the round trip records such units in test/repr/cpp/roundtrip-oracle.edn's :fails)"
  :decided "CX9 (review 196)"
  :match [#"^fail crash: cxxdump status 3: cxxdump: clang failed \(not a rejection\): signal: "]}
 {:id :pp-diag
  :title "the shared preprocessor does not give cpplib's diagnostic"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "cpplib's checks the port (arbace.repr.c.pp in C++ mode, CX2) does not make: identifiers' UCNs and UTF-8 that are invalid or not in NFC (-Winvalid-utf8, normalize), #elifdef and #warning before C++23 under -pedantic-errors, extra tokens after #endif, the module directives' macro rules (cpp-7 .. cpp-20), a #line number with digit separators, the 'began here' note gcc gives as an error"
  :decided "CX9; CX2 (pp-check judges the tokens, not the diagnostics)"
  :match [#"^fail pp-(missed|both|stopped): "]}
 {:id :pp-extra
  :title "the shared preprocessor errs where cpplib does not"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "an #error or a __has_include answer of a header the host data does not hold, a diagnostic the port gives as an error that cpplib gives as a warning"
  :decided "CX9; CX2"
  :match [#"^fail pp-extra: "]}
]
